  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Patchy river valley fog possible across the this \nmorning, otherwise dry through the rest of today.\n\nOutside of a few patches of high level cirrus, mainly clear skies \ngrace the area this morning. The clear skies is supporting \nradiational cooling, which may allow some limited fog to develop \nwithin the river valleys of the Southern Tier and the Black River. \n\nClear skies will then last today, supporting another beautiful \nsummer day, with temperatures spanning the 70s. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather returns Thursday, with increasing \nchances for showers and thunderstorms. Marginal risk for Severe \nThunderstorms across the western Southern Tier Thursday.\n\nA shortwave will drop east-southeast through the longwave trough \noverhead of the Great Lakes Thursday, supporting increased chances \nfor showers and thunderstorms. While the timing of the shortwave is \nstarting to iron itself out, there continues to remain some \nuncertainties. Model guidance continues to depict a leading \nshortwave trough just ahead of the stronger shortwave trough passing \nacross the upper Great Lakes. This being said, a weak warm frontal \nsegment will pass across the Great Lakes Thursday morning, which may \nkeep much of the area covered by a blanket of cloud cover with some \nembedded showers. The morning cloud cover and embedded showers will \nthen impact the amount of instability that will be able to build \nacross the area in the afternoon/evening hours ahead of a cold \nfrontal passage, where 00Z packages are now highlighting around 1000 \nJ/kg (a few pockets of 1500 J/kg across the western Southern Tier) \nof CAPE which will support a few thunderstorms that will have the \npotential to become strong to severe; there is a Marginal Risk for \nSevere Thunderstorms across the western Southern Tier.\n\nIn the wake of the frontal passage Thursday evening, expect shower \nand storm coverage to diminish from west to east overnight. A few \nstray showers will linger into Friday as the front slows and \neventually stalls out near the Mid-Atlantic region.",
